## Current source findings

### HAVEN

- `haven/CMakeLists.txt` lists only `qemu-virt`, `imx95-devkit`, and
  `imx8qm-mek` as target platforms. `haven/CMakePresets.json` exposes matching
  QEMU, i.MX95, i.MX8QM, and host-test presets. There is no CIX or Orange Pi
  platform implementation in `haven/src/platform/` at this revision.
- `src/platform/qemu-virt/platform.c` supplies fixed QEMU UART, GIC, and SMMU
  addresses and reports four CPUs. This is a QEMU binding, not a CIX hardware
  map.
- `include/haven/platform.h` exposes fixed base-address callbacks, timer
  frequency, and CPU count. The CIX firmware handoff, memory map, interrupt
  topology, requester IDs, and SMMU stream configuration are not yet bound by
  a target platform implementation.
- The existing architecture and evaluation documents still describe i.MX95
  as the primary physical target. Treat those claims as historical for the four
  named CIX boards until source and experiment evidence is reviewed.
- `arch/arm64/cpu.c::hv_arch_cpu_init()` documents that it must run once per
  CPU and configures the current CPU's EL2 state, including exception vectors.
  It also sets HCR_EL2, VTCR_EL2, ICC_SRE_EL2, and ICH_HCR_EL2/ICH_VMCR_EL2.
  `src/core/init.c::haven_init()` calls it for the primary CPU. The current
  `partition_b_secondary_cpu_init()` path enables B's stage-2 map and ERETs
  without calling that per-CPU initializer. A controlled QEMU comparison in a
  temporary copy supports this as a secondary-core startup defect: installing
  only vectors produced an EL2 fatal at B's entry; calling the full initializer
  produced CPU 2's two expected stage-2 denials from B. The run does not isolate
  which individual EL2 registers are necessary. A narrowed temporary setup
  (vectors, `HCR_VM | HCR_RW`, `VTCR_HAVEN_DEFAULT`, and existing VTTBR
  activation) produced the same stage-2 denials, so it is sufficient for this
  QEMU guest-entry/denial path. `hv_arch_gic_el2_setup()` is also called from
  `hv_el2_exceptions_init()` on the primary path, although its source comment
  says it is called once per CPU. The secondary test did not exercise guest
  interrupts; audit this GIC setup contract before claiming CPU 2 IRQ
  readiness. See the [secondary-core QEMU evidence](../haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261024-tfa-qemu-secondary/README.md).

### KDAL

- `kdal/src/backends/generic/platdev.c` currently has a no-op
  `platdev_backend_enumerate()` with a comment that real hardware should walk
  Device Tree nodes. The present generic backend does not yet provide the
  claimed hardware discovery path.
- KDAL's QEMU backend and interfaces can be evaluated as candidate software,
  but the source does not make it a HAVEN-mediated device assignment boundary.
  Do not connect it to real devices until HAVEN owns admission, MMIO/IRQ/DMA
  assignment, and revocation rules.

### ELMOS

- The macOS host has the Go, Task, CMake, Ninja, and ARM64 QEMU tools available.
- `elmos/Taskfile.yml` shows that `task dev:check` formats source, runs lint,
  and tests; it modifies files and is not a read-only inventory command. No such
  command was run.

## Host and virtual baseline follow-up (2026-10-23–24)

### ELMOS build and doctor check

- `task build` first reported the existing binary up to date. `task --force
  build` rebuilt the CLI from revision
  `5ef5cd7fb974fca961e1af7f5a7f08ff47f70e95`; the binary reports build commit
  `5ef5cd7` and Go 1.27.3.
- `./build/elmos doctor` completed with “All checks passed!” for required
  checks, including Homebrew packages, custom headers, and QEMU. Optional
  debugger/compiler checks and `crosstool-ng` were missing. The doctor checks
  for `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu-gcc`, while this host has
  `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc` and `aarch64-elf-gcc`; HAVEN's cross-build selected
  `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc` successfully. This is an optional tool-name mismatch,
  not a missing compiler for the observed build.
- The doctor ran without `--fix`. No ELMOS tests, dependency setup, or header
  downloads were run.

### HAVEN ARM64 build and QEMU experiment

- The standard `arm64-qemu` preset could not configure `haven/build`: its
  pre-existing CMake cache refers to `/Users/user/Documents/haven`,
  while the current source is under `Documents/workspace/haven`. The existing
  cache and old generated binaries were left untouched.
- An isolated build in `/private/tmp/haven-stage0-qemu` completed all 32 Ninja
  steps using CMake 4.4.4 and `aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc` 15.2.0. The linker warned
  about RWX LOAD segments in all three ELF images and a discarded GNU build-id
  note.
- QEMU 11.1.2 ran `virt,virtualization=on,gic-version=3,iommu=smmuv3`, Cortex-A72,
  4 CPUs, 2 GiB, loading the fresh hypervisor and partition images. It reached
  the HAVEN banner, mapped both partitions, assigned the A IRQ, set budgets,
  then entered the EL2 fatal handler. Neither guest marker appeared.
- A diagnostic-only change in `src/core/exc/el2_exceptions.c` now prints ESR,
  FAR, and ELR before the existing halt. A second QEMU run recorded
  `ESR=0x5a000000`, `FAR=0`, `ELR=0x800000e8`. ESR EC is `0x16` (HVC instruction
  exception); disassembly maps ELR to the instruction after `hvc #0` at
  `0x800000e4` in `hv_arch_psci_cpu_on()` (`arch/arm64/boot.S`). The immediate
  trigger is the PSCI CPU_ON HVC while HAVEN runs at EL2. Arm's guide says HVC
  targets EL2 and exceptions cannot reduce privilege; an HVC issued at EL2 is
  therefore taken at the current level. See the [Armv8-A ISA guide](https://developer.arm.com/-/media/Arm%20Developer%20Community/PDF/Learn%20the%20Architecture/Armv8-A%20Instruction%20Set%20Architecture.pdf),
  section 12.
- The replacement PSCI/secondary CPU contract remains unresolved. The current
  direct QEMU loader run shows no higher-level PSCI service, and no physical
  board firmware is available to check. TF-A's official QEMU virt guide
  documents a possible EL3/BL31 PSCI boot chain and generated PSCI FDT node;
  this is a candidate for a separate virtual baseline, not an observed property
  of the present direct-loader run or any physical target. Do not replace HVC
  with SMC or claim CPU_ON support until the QEMU and physical firmware
  handoffs are established. See the [TF-A QEMU virt guide](https://trustedfirmware-a.readthedocs.io/en/latest/plat/qemu.html)
  and [current TF-A porting guide](https://trustedfirmware-a.readthedocs.io/en/latest/porting-guide.html).
- A separate experiment built official TF-A `v2.15.0` at commit
  `da738d5eae93af342fdc4995dd3c05acb4c9d757` with QEMU's GICv3 driver and a
  preloaded BL33 address of `0x80000000`. A first setup attempt omitted the two
  guest image loader devices; its high-volume output is explicitly marked
  invalid for guest evaluation. In a corrected run, a clean temporary HAVEN
  copy changed the PSCI instruction from HVC to SMC; no tracked call-path change
  was made. With TF-A BL1/BL2/BL31, QEMU booted HAVEN at EL2, PSCI CPU_ON for
  CPU 2 returned success, and partition A printed its greeting, took its
  deliberate cross-partition stage-2 fault at IPA `0x60000000`, then printed
  its post-fault marker. This validates that narrow guest-A path under the
  specified virtual setup. It does not establish CPU 2's execution of guest B,
  DMA/SMMU isolation, or physical-board behavior. See the [initial TF-A setup
  trial](../haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261023-tfa-qemu/README.md) and the
  [corrected TF-A QEMU run](../haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261023-tfa-qemu-guest1/README.md).
- On 2026-10-24, a separate temporary-build comparison explicitly probed CPU
  2. A guest-B diagnostic load of A's IPA at B's entry was denied twice by
  stage-2 after calling `hv_arch_cpu_init()` on CPU 2; the logged CPU, ELR, IPA,
  FAR, and ESR identify the access path. A vectors-only control instead logged
  an EL2 fatal at ELR `0x60000000`. The source change was not applied to the
  workspace repository. The comparison supports incomplete per-core EL2 setup
  as the cause but does not isolate a single register or verify physical
  firmware. Full logs and image hashes are in the [secondary-core evidence
  record](../haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261024-tfa-qemu-secondary/README.md).
- A subsequent temporary variant replaced the full initializer with exception
  vectors, `HCR_VM | HCR_RW`, `VTCR_HAVEN_DEFAULT`, and the existing VTTBR
  activation. It produced the same two CPU 2 denied accesses and A's
  post-fault marker. This is the narrowest sufficient subset tested for this
  stage-2 smoke path, not a complete secondary-core init contract: GIC virtual
  interrupts, physical IRQ routing, timer state, cache state, and guest SMC
  behavior were not exercised. No tracked CPU-init change was made.
- TF-A's `fip` tool build was not needed for its documented semihosting flow.
  The initial `all fip` attempt stopped because the system OpenSSL headers were
  not available at TF-A's default `/usr` path. TF-A `all` then built BL1, BL2,
  and BL31 successfully using GNU Make 4.4.1 and GNU sed; macOS default make
  3.81 and BSD sed were incompatible with this source revision. No packages
  were installed.
- Raw UART/console logs and exact images are preserved in
  `haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261023-qemu/` and
  `haven/build/evidence/stage0-20261023-qemu-diag1/`. The source change is the
  only tracked HAVEN modification.
- No unit or integration suite was run. The direct-load QEMU baseline still
  fails at its EL2 PSCI HVC. The TF-A-backed temporary experiments observed
  guest A and guest B stage-2 denials after temporary PSCI and secondary-core
  setup changes. These are narrow virtual observations, not physical-target
  evidence or a tracked implementation fix.

